Recent breakthroughs in neural architecture search (NAS) have sparked significant interest, but a significant challenge remains to overcome the phenomenon of interference, where similar architectures with minor variations are mixed and matched by the search process, often resulting in identical or similar outcomes. Interference hampers NAS method performance and accuracy assessments, suppressing architectural innovations and misleading users about reported results. To address this issue, we first analyze and mathematically prove the root causes of interference in current NAS methods. Next, we introduce two straightforward techniques that alleviate interference: architectural smoothing and path smoothing. Our experiments demonstrate that these methods effectively mitigate interference, leading to superior search outcomes with varied architectures on multiple datasets, and highlight their potential to enhance NAS method performance and accuracy evaluations. An associated project page is available at: \url{https://towardsdatascience.com/tagged/neural-architecture-search}. Moreover, our code can be found on \url{https://github.com/andrebergamo/nas_search_path_smoothing}. Lastly, an earlier version of this paper was presented at the 2021 IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR). \copyright 2021 IEEE. \cite{bergamo2021towards} ightsquare  (CVPR 2021 LIPs Best Paper Candidate) \copyright 2021 IEEE. \cite{bergamo2021towards} ightsquare 
